  "rationale": "All three outputs are honest, read-only audits that do not claim any tools were run, and none turns the task into a redesign. C wins because it pairs every finding with explicit acceptance conditions and its validation plan is the most quantified: mounted-row caps, p95 latency, long-task, CLS and heap budgets, regression tolerances and repeat counts. That gives C the highest scores for concrete production fixes and measurement-first performance. B is close behind, with a clean table of failure states and careful detector nuance, but its performance gates are thinner. A is thorough but places polish before optimization and gives less concrete acceptance for each fix.",
  "recorded_at": "2026-10-12T04:15:26Z",
